  "summary": "An architectural style that romanticizes Polynesian and other Pacific Island cultures through decorative elements and themed design.",
  "description": "It features thatched roofs, bamboo elements, carved tiki statues, and other tropical motifs that created an exotic escape for American consumers during the mid-20th century.",
  "culturalContext": "Tiki architecture — the American Polynesian-pop fantasy of 1934 (Don the Beachcomber) through the 1960s — built restaurants and apartment blocks from A-frame carved tiki poles, thatch, bamboo, lava rock and moai statuary, inventing a hallucinatory \"South Pacific\" for postwar leisure (the Mai-Kai, Kahiki, Tiki-Ti). Designer Eli Hedley and artist shaper Bob Van Oosting shaped the canon; the style was critiqued for exoticism and revived since the 1990s (Sven Kirsten's Tiki Modern books) as mid-century Americana's most immersive escapist architecture.",
